[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - The perturbed node is **B**.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - We need to determine which nodes are descendants of B in the graph.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m **Edges**: B->C, B->D, D->A
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - B is a parent of C and D → C and D are descendants of B.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - D is a parent of A → A is a descendant of D.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m So, descendants of B are C and D. A is not a descendant of B.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m Thus, perturbation of B leads to:
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - A: 0 (not a descendant of B)
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - B: 0 (perturbed)
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - C: 1 (descendant of B)
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m - D: 1 (descendant of B)
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=462400)[0m This matches the observation: **Perturb(B) -> A:1 B:0 C:1 D:1** → **A:1, C:1, D:1** → **True**.
